# Lab08 — Azure SQL Database / PaaS

## Goal

* Learn Azure SQL Database as a PaaS service
* Understand PaaS vs IaaS
* Configure firewall access
* Configure Private Endpoint and Private DNS
* Disable public network access
* Understand backup, availability, and responsibility boundaries

---

## 1. Resource Group

```bash
RG="lab08-rg"
LOC="northcentralus"

az group create \
  --name "$RG" \
  --location "$LOC"
```

Resource Group:

* Name: `lab08-rg`
* Location: `northcentralus`

---

## 2. Register Azure SQL Resource Provider

```bash
az provider register --namespace Microsoft.Sql
```

Verified that the provider became:

```text
Registered
```

### What I learned

Registering `Microsoft.Sql` makes SQL resources available to the subscription.

It does **not** create a SQL Server.

---

## 3. Create SQL Logical Server

`northcentralus` was unavailable for creating a new Azure SQL logical server, so I used `westus`.

```bash
SQL_SERVER="lab08-sql-74518410"
SQL_ADMIN="sqladmin"

az sql server create \
  --resource-group "$RG" \
  --name "$SQL_SERVER" \
  --location "westus" \
  --admin-user "$SQL_ADMIN" \
  --admin-password "$SQL_PASSWORD"
```

SQL Server:

```text
lab08-sql-74518410
```

FQDN:

```text
lab08-sql-74518410.database.windows.net
```

---

## 4. Create Azure SQL Database

```bash
SQL_DB="lab08-db"

az sql db create \
  --resource-group "$RG" \
  --server "$SQL_SERVER" \
  --name "$SQL_DB" \
  --service-objective Basic
```

Database:

```text
lab08-db
```

Tier:

```text
Basic
```

Status:

```text
Online
```

### PaaS structure

```text
Azure SQL Logical Server
        │
        └── lab08-db
```

Unlike a VM-based database:

```text
VM
 ├── Operating System
 ├── SQL Server
 └── Database
```

With Azure SQL Database, Azure manages the underlying OS and platform.

---

## 5. Firewall

I checked my public IP from Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
(Invoke-RestMethod -Uri "https://api.ipify.org")
```

Then created a firewall rule:

```bash
MY_IP="38.172.226.131"

az sql server firewall-rule create \
  --resource-group "$RG" \
  --server "$SQL_SERVER" \
  --name "Allow-My-PC" \
  --start-ip-address "$MY_IP" \
  --end-ip-address "$MY_IP"
```

Firewall rule:

```text
Allow-My-PC
```

### What I learned

The firewall controls access through the **public SQL endpoint**.

However, after disabling public network access later, this rule is no longer useful for public access.

